<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8" /><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" /><title>Motion Tracking &amp; Thermal Studies | Daniel Winfield</title><meta name="description" content="Daniel Winfield’s Ni Lab research in 2D tracking, stereo reconstruction, and fabrication of digital-composite cavity-size variants." /><link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles/research.css" /><link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles/evidence.css" /><SiteHead title={"Motion Tracking & Thermal Studies | Daniel Winfield"} description={"Daniel Winfield’s Ni Lab research in 2D tracking, stereo reconstruction, and fabrication of digital-composite cavity-size variants."} path={"/research/thermal-diffusion/"} />
</head><body id="top"><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><SiteHeader section="research" /><main id="main"><section class="research-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Experimental Research · Computer Vision · Fabrication</p><h1>Motion Tracking &amp; Thermal Studies</h1><p class="subtitle">Digital Composites in the Ni Lab</p><p class="hero-intro">Developing measurement workflows for soft robotic motion and fabricating cavity-size variants for a single-voxel heating study.</p><div class="research-meta"><span>Duke University</span><span>Jan. 2026 – Present</span><span>Graduate researcher</span></div><p class="status">Ongoing · Stereo fixture evaluated; new thermal specimens await testing</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#contributions">My contributions ↓</a><a class="button" href="/documents/research/ni-lab-poster.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View research poster (PDF)</a></div></div><figure class="evidence-figure portrait"><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/fixture-camera-b.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Stereo evaluation fixture at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/fixture-camera-b.png" alt="Stereo evaluation fixture" width="1080" height="1920" loading="eager" /></a><figcaption><strong>Stereo evaluation fixture</strong><span>A marked fixture used to compare reconstructed geometry with nominal dimensions.</span></figcaption></figure></section>
<nav class="page-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#contributions">My contributions</a><a href="#tracking">2D tracking</a><a href="#stereo">Stereo reconstruction</a><a href="#thermal">Voxel fabrication</a><a href="#poster">Poster &amp; sources</a></nav>
<section class="section dark" id="contributions"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>My Contributions</h2></div><p>Connecting physical experiments with image analysis and measured geometry.</p></div><div class="contribution-list"><div><span>01</span><p>Tracked fluorescent markers on robotic-tail samples and compared image-plane motion across eight recordings.</p></div><div><span>02</span><p>Developed a stereo workflow using camera calibration, synchronized recordings, rectification, and triangulation, then evaluated reconstructed fixture dimensions.</p></div><div><span>03</span><p>Designed and printed molds for nominal 3-, 4-, and 5-mm internal cavities, helped cast the variants in silicone, and assembled the 4-mm specimen with spin-coated caps.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="tracking"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>2D Motion Baseline</h2></div><p>Nine markers per sample; vibration-generator inputs of 6 or 12 Hz and 10 or 20 Vpp.</p></div><div class="evidence-grid"><figure class="evidence-figure portrait"><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/tail-tracking.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Fluorescent markers on a tail sample at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/tail-tracking.png" alt="Fluorescent markers on a tail sample" width="600" height="1078" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Fluorescent markers on a tail sample</strong><span>Actual tracking view used in the motion analysis.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/tracking-comparison.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Tip excursion across the recorded conditions at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/tracking-comparison.png" alt="Tip excursion across the recorded conditions" width="646" height="445" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Tip excursion across the recorded conditions</strong><span>Figure excerpt from the reviewed poster. Tip excursion, in pixels, is the 95th minus 5th percentile of detected horizontal displacement.</span></figcaption></figure></div><p class="evidence-summary">Both samples showed greater tip excursion at 6 Hz and at 20 Vpp in these recordings. This comparison established an image-plane baseline for the stereo workflow.</p><p class="media-note">One recording per sample and condition. Displacement is in pixels, and the vibration-generator Vpp is separate from heater voltage.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="stereo"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03</p><h2>Stereo Reconstruction</h2></div><p>Extending image-plane tracking to spatial coordinates.</p></div><p class="evidence-summary">Two Android phones observe the same scene. A shared light flash aligns the recordings, while ChArUco calibration estimates lens distortion and relative camera geometry. Rectification and triangulation convert matched image points into 3D coordinates.</p><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/stereo-workflow.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Stereo acquisition and processing workflow at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/stereo-workflow.png" alt="Stereo acquisition and processing workflow" width="1389" height="571" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Stereo acquisition and processing workflow</strong><span>Diagram excerpt from the reviewed poster. Camera positions remain fixed between calibration and measurement.</span></figcaption></figure><div class="evidence-grid three"><figure class="evidence-figure portrait"><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/fixture-camera-a.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Fixture viewed by camera A at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/fixture-camera-a.png" alt="Fixture viewed by camera A" width="1080" height="1920" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Fixture viewed by camera A</strong><span>The fixture is independent of the calibration board.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="evidence-figure portrait"><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/fixture-camera-b.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Fixture viewed by camera B at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/fixture-camera-b.png" alt="Fixture viewed by camera B" width="1080" height="1920" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Fixture viewed by camera B</strong><span>Corresponding pillar centers are identified in both views.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/fixture-reconstruction.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Reconstructed fixture coordinates at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/fixture-reconstruction.png" alt="Reconstructed fixture coordinates" width="1840" height="1564" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Reconstructed fixture coordinates</strong><span>P0 exceeds the 3 px epipolar threshold and is excluded from distance statistics.</span></figcaption></figure></div><div class="finding-grid evidence-findings"><article><span class="finding-number">7 / 8</span><h3>Landmarks accepted</h3><p>Screened points provide 21 pair distances in one fixture analysis.</p></article><article><span class="finding-number">3.22 mm</span><h3>Mean absolute distance error</h3><p>Compared with nominal distances; mean absolute percentage error was 6.03%. No scale correction was fitted.</p></article></div><p class="media-note">Calibration used 37 paired views with a stereo reprojection RMS of 0.93 px. These results describe one fixture evaluation; repeatability and moving-tail reconstruction remain further work.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="thermal"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04</p><h2>Voxel Fabrication</h2></div><p>Preparing cavity-size variants for follow-up heating and cooling measurements.</p></div><p class="evidence-summary">I designed and printed molds with nominal internal cavity dimensions of 3 × 3 × 5, 4 × 4 × 4, and 5 × 5 × 5 mm. We cast all three variants in Dragon Skin silicone and assembled the 4-mm-cavity specimen with spin-coated caps. These dimensions describe the internal cavities, rather than the complete specimen exterior.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Current status</strong><p>Thermal measurements of the new specimens are pending. Heating-rate, cooling, and geometry-matched FEA comparisons are planned.</p></div><div class="evidence-grid three"><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/prior-lab-setup.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Prior lab single-voxel setup at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/prior-lab-setup.png" alt="Prior lab single-voxel setup" width="478" height="387" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Prior lab single-voxel setup</strong><span>Earlier lab specimen and thermometer setup supplied by Yang. This is background for the measurement pipeline.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/cavity-top.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Earlier lab voxel, top view at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/cavity-top.png" alt="Earlier lab voxel, top view" width="250" height="248" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Earlier lab voxel, top view</strong><span>Reference specimen photo supplied by Yang; not a photo of the newly assembled 4-mm-cavity specimen.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/cavity-side.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Earlier lab voxel, side view at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/cavity-side.png" alt="Earlier lab voxel, side view" width="259" height="248" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Earlier lab voxel, side view</strong><span>Reference specimen photo with the original 1 mm scale bar.</span></figcaption></figure></div><h3 class="evidence-subheading">Prior Lab Results</h3><p class="evidence-summary">Yang Weng and Yuxuan Guo provided the existing thermal experiments and model comparisons below. They establish the lab’s measurement approach and are separate from results for my new cavity-size variants.</p><div class="evidence-grid"><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/lab-constant-power.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Prior lab constant-power heating at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/lab-constant-power.png" alt="Prior lab constant-power heating" width="1408" height="892" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Prior lab constant-power heating</strong><span>Thermometer measurements at 102, 128, and 155 mW compared with FEA steady-state estimates. Results: Yang and Yuxuan.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/research/ni-lab/lab-thermal-control.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Prior lab closed-loop thermal control at full resolution"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/lab-thermal-control.png" alt="Prior lab closed-loop thermal control" width="1539" height="975" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Prior lab closed-loop thermal control</strong><span>Heater self-sensing and reference thermometer measurements compared with FEA using measured heater power as input. Results: Yang and Yuxuan.</span></figcaption></figure></div></section>
<section class="section" id="poster"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">05</p><h2>Research Poster</h2></div><p>The November 2026 poster brings the tracking, reconstruction, and heating studies together.</p></div><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="/documents/research/ni-lab-poster.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open poster (PDF)</a><a class="button" href="/documents/research/ni-lab-poster.pdf" download>Download poster</a></div><a class="poster-preview-link" href="/documents/research/ni-lab-poster.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/research/ni-lab/poster-preview.png" alt="Research poster: 3D Reconstruction and Voxel Heating in Digital Composites" width="1512" height="1296" loading="lazy" /></a><p class="credits">Research adviser: Dr. Xiaoyue Ni. Collaborators and acknowledgments: Yang Weng, Yuxuan Guo, and the Ni Lab. Thermal figures and earlier specimen photographs were supplied by Yang. Earlier work included PIV, flow visualization, and work with Yang on tail design, fabrication methods, and actuation testing.</p><p class="credits">Research background: Bai et al., <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aed9698" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><cite>Digital composites with reprogrammable phase architectures</cite></a>. Full figure credits and study limitations appear in the poster.</p></section><nav class="research-navigation" aria-label="More research"><a href="/research/"><span>Back to</span><strong>All Research ↑</strong></a><a href="/research/bes-defects/"><span>Next research</span><strong>Cubic BeS Defects →</strong></a></nav></main><SiteFooter /></body></html>